  "elements": [
    {
      "id": "EzU2rSsF6UvJxsVD8-lHY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 197.80977741042875,
      "y": -562.0272392587428,
      "width": 1743.0904395668047,
      "height": 2119.05949473746,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1757575020,
      "version": 190,
      "versionNonce": 643059436,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1742588129077,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Functional Requirements:\n\nUser can upload a file\nUser can download a file\nSync a file across multiple devices\n\nNon functional requirements:\nLow latency uploads and downloads\nHigh availability\nEventual Consistency (availability > consistency) -> eg: its fine to sync data with a delay of 15s\nSupport large file uploads\n\nBack of the envelope calculation:\n\n100M DAU\nwrites: 200M file uploads -> 200M/86400 -> 2k rps -> 10k rps\nreads: \nSyncs file data -> 10k rps\nMetadata 1:2 -> 20k rps\nStorage\n200M * 1 GB -> 200 PB -> 200PB * 365  * 10 ~ 7 EB ~ think of cold storage solution for old records\n\nCore entities:\n1. File\n2. FileChunk\n3. User\n\nNoSQL -> Don't need consistency or transactional guarantees. There are going to be billions of file uploads \nwhich NoSQL can handle because they scale horizontally. Apart from this, there is not a sure schema for this kind of data. NoSQL\nallows schema change without DB migrations.\n\nAPIs:\n\nPOST /api/v1/file\nrequest header -> Content-Type: multipart-upload\nrequest body\n{\n    fileMetadata: { mimeType, sizeBytes }\n    FileBinary\n}\n\nresponse ->\n{\n    \"preSignedUrl\": \"https://s3....\"\n}\n\n2. PUT /api/v1/file/{fileId}/file-chunk/{fileChunkId}\n{\n    \"filePath\": \"\",\n    \"status\": \"uploaded\"\n}\n\n3. POST /api/v1/file/{fileId}/file-chunk\n{\n    \"filePath\": \"\",\n    \"status\": \"uploaded\"\n}\n\n4. GET /api/v1/file/{fileId} -> \"status\", file chunk details\n\n5. GET /api/v1/file/{fileId}/changes -> returns file chunks that have changed\n\n6. GET /api/v1/file/{fileId}/file-chunk/{fileChunkId} -> status: \"uploading\" | \"available\"",
      "fontSize": 26.908691996666164,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Functional Requirements:\n\nUser can upload a file\nUser can download a file\nSync a file across multiple devices\n\nNon functional requirements:\nLow latency uploads and downloads\nHigh availability\nEventual Consistency (availability > consistency) -> eg: its fine to sync data with a delay of 15s\nSupport large file uploads\n\nBack of the envelope calculation:\n\n100M DAU\nwrites: 200M file uploads -> 200M/86400 -> 2k rps -> 10k rps\nreads: \nSyncs file data -> 10k rps\nMetadata 1:2 -> 20k rps\nStorage\n200M * 1 GB -> 200 PB -> 200PB * 365  * 10 ~ 7 EB ~ think of cold storage solution for old records\n\nCore entities:\n1. File\n2. FileChunk\n3. User\n\nNoSQL -> Don't need consistency or transactional guarantees. There are going to be billions of file uploads \nwhich NoSQL can handle because they scale horizontally. Apart from this, there is not a sure schema for this kind of data. NoSQL allows schema change without DB migrations.\n\nAPIs:\n\nPOST /api/v1/file\nrequest header -> Content-Type: multipart-upload\nrequest body\n{\n    fileMetadata: { mimeType, sizeBytes }\n    FileBinary\n}\n\nresponse ->\n{\n    \"preSignedUrl\": \"https://s3....\"\n}\n\n2. PUT /api/v1/file/{fileId}/file-chunk/{fileChunkId}\n{\n    \"filePath\": \"\",\n    \"status\": \"uploaded\"\n}\n\n3. POST /api/v1/file/{fileId}/file-chunk\n{\n    \"filePath\": \"\",\n    \"status\": \"uploaded\"\n}\n\n4. GET /api/v1/file/{fileId} -> \"status\", file chunk details\n\n5. GET /api/v1/file/{fileId}/changes -> returns file chunks that have changed\n\n6. GET /api/v1/file/{fileId}/file-chunk/{fileChunkId} -> status: \"uploading\" | \"available\"",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
